  "title": "Hold, Hang, Carry and Pour: ten, pend, fer and fund",
  "short": "ten means hold, pend means hang or weigh, fer means carry and fund and fus mean pour.",
  "trouble": "tain and tin are the same root as ten (contain, abstain); pensive comes from weighing thoughts.",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.ab-ad-pro"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "To <i>contain</i> is to hold together, and a <i>tenant</i> holds a home. <b>ten</b> (also <b>tain</b> and <b>tin</b>) means “hold.” A <i>pendulum</i> hangs and swings: <b>pend</b> (also <b>pens</b>) means “hang” or “weigh.” To <i>transfer</i> is to carry across: <b>fer</b> means “carry.” And a <i>refund</i> pours money back: <b>fund</b> (also <b>fus</b>) means “pour.”"
      },
